# Reasoning cycle

The **reasoning cycle** is the loop in which observations lead to a
hypothesis, the hypothesis leads to predictions, and testing the predictions
leads to new observations. It is the smallest unit in which [[induction]] and
[[deduction]] make sense, because each supplies what the other lacks. It is a
[[feedback-loop]], and by itself it is nothing more than that.

## Description

### The cycle

```mermaid
flowchart TD
 A[Observations] -->|Induction / abduction| B[Hypotheses or model]
 B -->|Deduction| C[Predictions]
 C -->|Testing| D[New observations]
 D --> A
```

The upward arc, from observations to a model, is taken by [[induction]] when
there are many cases and by [[abduction]] when there is one surprising case.
The downward arc, from the model to its consequences, is [[deduction]]. The
return, from consequences to the world, is testing, which is a form of
[[evaluation]]. What comes back is used for [[revision]] of the model.

The cycle was drawn in the discussion at the moment induction was restored to
the list of operations. Its point there was that the three inference methods
are not a list of alternatives. They are positions on one loop.

### The same cycle in other clothes

The discussion and the essay draw this loop four times, with different labels.

| Where | Upward arc | Downward arc | Return |
|---|---|---|---|
| Scientific inference | induction, abduction | deduction | testing |
| Compression | discover regularities, build model | predict outcomes | choose action and evaluate |
| Theories | select new axioms | prove, inspect consequences | reflect on the theory and its limits |
| AI-assisted programming | intention to specification | program to execution | observations to revised specification |

The last row is the chain *natural-language intention, formal specification,
generated program, execution, observations, revised specification*. Its
downward arc is what a conventional program does well, and its upward arc is
where a language model is useful. The line between executing an established
formalization and discovering or revising one runs between the two arcs.

### The second loop

Two of the drawings in the discussion have a second, outer loop. In the
compression version the evaluation step feeds a box labelled "build a model of
the modeling process", which feeds back into the discovery of regularities. In
the theory version, reflection on the theory feeds "reflect on criteria for
revision", which changes how new axioms are selected.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
 A[Observations] --> B[Model]
 B --> C[Predictions]
 C --> D[Evaluate]
 D --> A
 D --> E[Model of the modelling process]
 E --> B
```

The inner loop revises the model. The outer loop revises the way models are
made. This is where [[reflection]] enters, and it is the difference between a
cycle that merely runs and one that could become a [[strange-loop]]: only the
outer loop passes through a representation of the cycle itself. A reasoning
cycle without the outer loop is ordinary feedback however many times it turns.

## Relations

- It is a [[feedback-loop]] {relation: is-a}: its output, new observations, becomes its next input.
- It is not by itself a [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: every turn stays on the level of the model and its data.
- Its upward arc is [[induction]] {relation: uses} or, for a single surprising case, [[abduction]] {relation: uses}.
- Its downward arc is [[deduction]] {relation: uses}.
- Its consequences are [[prediction]] {relation: uses}, and its return step is [[evaluation]] {relation: uses}.
- Each turn ends in [[revision]] {relation: uses} of the model.
- Its outer loop is [[reflection]] {relation: uses}: a model of the modelling process.
- It is the pattern behind [[compression-and-prediction]] {relation: generalizes} and behind the [[hmd-feedback-loop]] {relation: generalizes}.
- Applied to theories and their axioms it is a [[reflection-progression]] {relation: analogous-to}.
- It arranges the [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: uses} list into an order of use.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}: the experiment has to implement the outer loop, not only the inner one.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[induction]].** Inference from observations to a general hypothesis. In
  the cycle it is the arc that produces something for deduction to work on.
- **[[deduction]].** Inference from premises to what must follow. In the cycle
  it turns a hypothesis into predictions that can fail.
- **[[feedback-loop]].** Output influencing later input, with no change of
  level. The reasoning cycle is the instance whose signal is evidence.
- **[[reflection]].** Analysis of a process by the system that runs it. It is
  the outer loop of the cycle, and the only part that looks at the cycle.
- **[[revision]].** Updating a model in the light of evidence. It closes each
  turn, and in the outer loop it is applied to the rules of revision too.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[reasoning-cycle]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[reasoning-cycle]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

Future lens that applies to this card: [[find-level-crossings]] would have to
tell the inner loop from the outer one in any cycle it finds.

